{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n344 closed bugs exist but no lens aggregates them: close_class distribution over time, detection channel, open-bug age, caused_by fan-in (which components spawn bugs), time-to-close. Trend questions are answered today by ad-hoc scripts.\n\n## Approach\nAdd a bug_trends lens to the rebar metrics registry (same _spec() registration pattern as gate-economics sidecar metrics). Dimensions: close_class distribution bucketed by close month; detected_by distribution (populated as the detected-by sibling lands — lens must degrade gracefully while the field is sparse); open-bug age percentiles; caused_by link fan-in per target; time-to-close percentiles. Surface taxonomy-window skew INSIDE the computed value payload: the close_class distribution carries an explicit labeled MISSING bucket (the pre-convention era where close_class is absent for ~214 bugs) alongside the vocabulary classes, so eras are never silently mixed. (The registry source/confidence labels are closed spec-level vocabularies and cannot express per-cohort annotations; each spec registers as source=structural, confidence=high.)\n\nRange semantics (stock vs flow): the FLOW dimensions — close_class-by-month and time-to-close — filter on the close TRANSITION timestamp within the ctx since/until range, exactly as sibling event/sidecar metrics filter on their ranges; with the CLI default 30-day window the historical cohorts only appear when the caller widens --since (documented in the user-guide section). The STOCK dimensions — open-bug age percentiles, detected_by distribution, caused_by fan-in — are point-in-time measures over the current store and deliberately ignore the range (an age percentile or fan-in count has no flow window); this deviation from range-filtering siblings is stated here and in the module docstring.\n\n## Scope\n- src/rebar/metrics/bug_trends.py (new lens module; register() appends specs idempotently on id)\n- src/rebar/metrics/__init__.py (side-effect import of the new module alongside event_metrics/git_metrics/sidecar_metrics — without it REGISTRY never sees the lens)\n- docs/user-guide.md metrics section\n\n## Testing\nHappy path: synthetic store with classed+unclassed bugs -> lens returns distributions with era-skew labels. Edge: zero bugs -> 'unavailable' state per registry convention, not a crash or empty-implies-healthy. detected_by absent everywhere -> dimension reports unavailable, other dimensions unaffected.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] 'rebar metrics' exposes a bug_trends lens with the five dimensions, registered via the standard registry pattern.\n- [ ] Pre-convention MISSING close_class is surfaced as a labeled cohort, never silently dropped or merged.\n- [ ] Sparse/absent detected_by degrades that dimension to unavailable without affecting others.\n- [ ] Unit tests cover populated, empty, and mixed-era stores."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785729459141406001,"uuid":"c8fc4ecd-353f-4049-a9a1-fe36129eab99"}